/-
# No countable family of groups generates all varieties (Kourovka Notebook Problem 4.47)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.47 (A. Yu. Olshanskii): does there exist a
countable family of groups such that every variety is generated by a subfamily of it?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import WantedExt.GroupTheory.FormationVarietyPGroupsKourovka11Problem21Wanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.CountableFamilyGeneratingVarietiesKourovka4Problem47Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.47 (A. Yu.
Olshanskii). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700254 (`KOU-4.47`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A countable family is a sequence `(Fᵢ)_{i ∈ ℕ}` of groups (finite families repeat a member). Laws
are words in `FreeGroup ℕ`, and `SatisfiesLaws W G` (of the Kourovka 11.21 entry, reused here) says
every `w ∈ W` maps to `1` under every homomorphism to `G`. Every variety is the class of groups
satisfying some set `L` of laws, and the variety generated by `(Fᵢ)_{i ∈ S}` is the class of groups
satisfying every law common to all `Fᵢ`, `i ∈ S`; the target compares the two classes over all
groups in `Type`.
-/

open MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FormationVarietyPGroupsKourovka11Problem21Wanted

/-- Kourovka 4.47 (A. Yu. Olshanskii): there is a countable family `(Fᵢ)` of groups such that
every variety of groups is generated by a subfamily: for every set `L` of laws (defining the
variety of groups satisfying `L`) some subfamily `(Fᵢ)_{i ∈ S}` generates that variety, that is,
the groups satisfying `L` are exactly the groups satisfying every law common to all `Fᵢ`,
`i ∈ S`. -/
def existsCountableFamilyGeneratingAllVarieties : Prop :=
  ∃ (F : ℕ → Type) (_ : ∀ i, Group (F i)), ∀ L : Set (FreeGroup ℕ), ∃ S : Set ℕ,
    ∀ (H : Type) [Group H],
      SatisfiesLaws L H ↔ SatisfiesLaws {w | ∀ i ∈ S, SatisfiesLaws {w} (F i)} H

/--
Resolved false: Kleĭman shows that no countable family of groups generates every variety of groups
by its subfamilies; the Kourovka Archive answers 4.47 "No". Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.47 (A. Yu. Olshanskii); resolution: Yu. G. Kleĭman,
Some questions in the theory of varieties of groups, Math. USSR-Izv. 22 (1984), 33-65, Kourovka
Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 4.47: No, https://doi.org/10.1070/IM1984v022n01ABEH001433.
-/
public theorem_wanted existsCountableFamilyGeneratingAllVarieties_refuted :
    ¬ existsCountableFamilyGeneratingAllVarieties

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.CountableFamilyGeneratingVarietiesKourovka4Problem47Wanted
